It turns out reflection could significantly improve the quality of its output. Let's take a look.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:26,300
In this example, I have data from a coffee machine showing when different drinks such as a latte coffee or hot chocolate or a cappuccino coffee and so on were sold and for what price.

4
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:33,600
And we want to have an agent create a plot comparing Q1 or first quarter coffee sales in 2024 and 2025.

5
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:41,100
So one way to do it would be to write a prompt that asks an LLM to create a plot comparing Q1 coffee sales in 2024 and 2025,

6
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:48,980
using the data stored in a spreadsheet as a CSV file or comma separated values as a spreadsheet file.

7
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:52,880
And an LLM might write Python code like this to generate the plot.

8
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:58,740
And with this V1 of the code, if you execute it, it may generate a plot like this.

9
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:03,260
When I ran the code that the LLM output actually generated this the first time.

10
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:11,300
And this is a stacked bar plot, which is not a very easy way to visualize things and it just doesn't look a very good plot.

11
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:20,620
But what you can do is then give it V1 of the code as well as the plot that this code generated and feed it into a multimodal model.

12
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:28,680
That is an LLM that can also accept image inputs and ask it to examine the image that was generated by this code.

13
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:36,460
And then to critique the image, find a way to come up with better visualization and update the code to just generate a clearer, better plot.

14
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:44,040
Multimodal LLMs can use visual reasoning so it can actually look visually at this figure to find ways to improve it.

15
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:58,020
And when I did this, it actually generated a bar graph that isn't this stacked bar graph, but a more regular bar graph that separates out the 2054 and 2055 coffee sales in what I thought was a more pleasing and clearer way.

16
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:04,420
When you get to the coding lab, please feel free to mess around with the problems and see if you can get maybe even better looking graphs in these.

17
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:11,540
Because different LLMs have different strengths and weaknesses, sometimes I'll use different LLMs for the initial generation and for the reflection.

18
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:25,200
So, for example, you may use one LLM to generate the initial code, maybe openize GPT-4 or GPT-5 or some model like that and just prompt it like a prompt like this to write Python code to generate visualization and so on.

19
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:43,680
And then the reflection prompt might be something like this where you tell the LLM to play the role of an expert data analyst that provides constructive feedback and then give it the version one of the code, the part that was generated, maybe also the conversational history from how the code was generated and ask it to critique it for specific criteria.

20
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:53,940
Remember, when we give it specific criteria like readability, clarity and completeness, it helps the LLM better figure out what to do and then ask it to write new code to implement your improvements.

21
00:02:53,940 --> 00:03:01,880
One thing you may find is that sometimes using a reasoning model for reflection may work better than a non-reasoning model.

22
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:11,900
So when you're trying out different models for the initial generation and the reflection, these are different configurations that you might toggle or try different combinations of.

